items.question:
why does this crash with a ConcurrentModificationException, and what's the cleanest fix?

List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));
for (Integer n : nums) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) {
        nums.remove(n);
    }
}
System.out.println(nums);

items.answer:
The for-each loop uses an iterator under the hood, and calling `nums.remove(n)` modifies the list directly instead of through that iterator. The iterator's next `next()` call detects the structural change and throws `ConcurrentModificationException`.

The cleanest fix is `removeIf`, which handles the iteration and removal safely:

```java
List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));
nums.removeIf(n -> n % 2 == 0);
System.out.println(nums);
```

If you need to keep the explicit loop, use `Iterator.remove()` instead, since that's the one removal the iterator knows about and accounts for.

```javascript
function isPalindrome(s) {
  const cleaned = s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
  let left = 0, right = cleaned.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    if (cleaned[left] !== cleaned[right]) return false;
    left++;
    right--;
  }
  return true;
}
```

The regex strips anything that isn't a letter or digit, `toLowerCase` handles case, then two pointers walk inward and bail on the first mismatch. Time O(n), space O(n) for the cleaned string.
